"To find the area of kite \\(ABCD\\), we first need to determine the lengths of its diagonals. The diagonals of a kite are the line segments connecting opposite vertices.\n\n1. Calculate the length of diagonal \\(AC\\):\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{(12-0)^2 + (-2-7)^2} = \\sqrt{12^2 + (-9)^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 81} = \\sqrt{225} = 15\n\\]\n\n2. Calculate the length of diagonal \\(BD\\):\n\\[\nBD = \\sqrt{(7-1)^2 + (8-0)^2} = \\sqrt{6^2 + 8^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 64} = \\sqrt{100} = 10\n\\]\n\nThe area of a kite is given by half the product of its diagonals:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 15 \\times 10 = 75\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of kite \\(ABCD\\) is \\(\\boxed{750}\\)."
"To solve the equation \\((17^6 - 17^5) \\div 16 = 17^x\\), we start by simplifying the left-hand side.\n\nFirst, factor out \\(17^5\\) from the numerator:\n\\[\n17^6 - 17^5 = 17^5(17 - 1) = 17^5 \\cdot 16\n\\]\n\nNext, divide the result by 16:\n\\[\n\\frac{17^5 \\cdot 16}{16} = 17^5\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation simplifies to:\n\\[\n17^5 = 17^x\n\\]\n\nSince the bases are the same, we can set the exponents equal to each other:\n\\[\n5 = x\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{5}\\)."
"Let $f$ be the cost of one pound of feathers and $g$ be the cost of one ounce of gold. We have the following system of equations:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n8f + 2g &= 932 \\\\\n14f + 3g &= 1402\n\\end{align*}\n\nWe can solve this system using substitution or elimination. Let's use elimination. Multiply the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 2:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n16f + 4g &= 1864 \\\\\n28f + 6g &= 2804\n\\end{align*}\n\nSubtract the first equation from the second:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n28f + 6g - (16f + 4g) &= 2804 - 1864 \\\\\n12f &= 940 \\\\\nf &= 70\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow, we can find the cost of five pounds of feathers and five ounces of gold:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n5f &= 5 \\cdot 70 & = \\boxed{350}\n\\end{align*}"
"We start with the given equation:\n\\[ x^2 + y^2 = 14x + 48y. \\]\n\nFirst, we complete the square for the \\(x\\) terms:\n\\[ x^2 - 14x = (x - 7)^2 - 49. \\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for the \\(y\\) terms:\n\\[ y^2 - 48y = (y - 24)^2 - 576. \\]\n\nSubstituting these into the original equation, we get:\n\\[ (x - 7)^2 - 49 + (y - 24)^2 - 576 = 0. \\]\n\nSimplifying, we have:\n\\[ (x - 7)^2 + (y - 24)^2 = 625. \\]\n\nThis is the equation of a circle with center \\((7, 24)\\) and radius \\(25\\). The maximum value of \\(y\\) is the coordinate of the center of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of \\(y\\) is \\(\\boxed{24}\\)."
"We start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{4a+3b}{a-2b} = 5\n\\]\n\nFirst, we cross-multiply to eliminate the fraction:\n\\[\n4a + 3b = 5(a - 2b)\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side:\n\\[\n4a + 3b = 5a - 10b\n\\]\n\nRearranging the terms to isolate \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[\n4a + 3b = 5a - 10b\n\\]\n\\[\n13b = a\n\\]\n\\[\na = 13b\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(a = 13b\\) into the expression \\(\\frac{a + 11b}{a-b}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{a + 11b}{a - b} = \\frac{13b + 11b}{13b - b} = \\frac{24b}{12b} = 12\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified numerical value of the expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]"
