"For the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + 2bx + (a-b) = 0\\) to have one real solution, its discriminant must be zero. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by \\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\).\n\nHere, \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 2b\\), and \\(c = a - b\\). Therefore, the discriminant is:\n\\[\n\\Delta = (2b)^2 - 4(1)(a - b) = 4b^2 - 4(a - b)\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have one real solution, the discriminant must be zero:\n\\[\n4b^2 - 4(a - b) = 0\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(a\\), we get:\n\\[\n4b^2 = 4(a - b)\n\\]\n\\[\nb^2 = a - b\n\\]\n\\[\na = b^2 + b\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a\\) is uniquely determined:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{b^2 + b}\n\\]"
"First, simplify the expression \\(\\dfrac{8^{-1}}{4^{-1}}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{1}{8} \\div \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{8} \\times \\dfrac{4}{1} = \\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{1}{2} - a^{-1} = 1\n\\]\n\nNext, isolate \\(a^{-1}\\) by subtracting \\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n-a^{-1} = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{2}{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nMultiply both sides by \\(-1\\) to solve for \\(a^{-1}\\):\n\n\\[\na^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nFinally, solve for \\(a\\) by taking the reciprocal of \\(a^{-1}\\):\n\n\\[\na = -2\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a\\) is \\(\\boxed{-2}\\)."
