"To solve the equation \\( |x + 3| = 6 \\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The equation \\( |x + 3| = 6 \\) can be split into two separate equations:\n\n1. \\( x + 3 = 6 \\)\n2. \\( x + 3 = -6 \\)\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n1. \\( x + 3 = 6 \\)\n   Subtract 3 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   x = 6 - 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 3\n   \\]\n\n2. \\( x + 3 = -6 \\)\n   Subtract 3 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   x = -6 - 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = -9\n   \\]\n\nThe solutions to the equation are \\( x = 3 \\) and \\( x = -9 \\). The positive difference between these solutions is:\n\\[\n3 - (-9) = |12| = 12\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the positive difference between the solutions is \\(\\boxed{12}\\)."
