"To determine the probability that the difference between the two numbers rolled on two fair, standard six-sided dice is 0, we need to consider the possible outcomes and the conditions under which the difference is 0.\n\nEach die has 6 faces, so the total number of possible outcomes when rolling two dice is:\n\\[ 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\]\n\nFor the difference between the two numbers rolled to be 0, both dice must show the same number. There are 6 possible numbers (1 through 6) that both dice can show. Therefore, there are 6 favorable outcomes.\n\nThe probability \\( P \\) of an event is given by the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of possible outcomes. Thus, the probability that the difference between the two numbers rolled is 0 is:\n\\[ P = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of possible outcomes}} = \\frac{6}{36} = \\frac{1}{6} \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}} \\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $\\frac{1}{6}$ correct."
