"To find the magnitude of the complex number \\(-1 + i\\sqrt{3}\\), we use the formula for the magnitude of a complex number \\(a + bi\\), which is given by \\(\\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}\\).\n\nHere, \\(a = -1\\) and \\(b = \\sqrt{3}\\).\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\n|-1 + i\\sqrt{3}| = \\sqrt{(-1)^2 + (\\sqrt{3})^2}\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\((-1)^2\\):\n\n\\[\n(-1)^2 = 1\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\((\\sqrt{3})^2\\):\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{3})^2 = 3\n\\]\n\nNow, add these results together:\n\n\\[\n1 + 3 = 4\n\\]\n\nFinally, take the square root of 4:\n\n\\[\n|-1+i| = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the complex number \\(-1 + i\\sqrt{3}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(2\\). I hope it is correct."
