"Given that points \\( A \\), \\( B \\), and \\( C \\) are vertices of three identical squares, and \\( AB = 2\\sqrt{5} \\) cm, we need to find the length of \\( AC \\).\n\nFirst, observe that \\( AB \\) is a side of one of the squares. Since \\( AB = 2\\sqrt{5} \\) cm, the side length of each square is \\( 2\\sqrt{5} \\) cm.\n\nNext, consider the triangle formed by \\( A \\), \\( B \\), and \\( C \\). Since \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) are vertices of two adjacent squares, \\( AC \\) is the diagonal of one of the squares. The diagonal \\( d \\) of a square with side length \\( s \\) is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ d = s\\sqrt{2} \\]\n\nSubstituting the side length \\( s = 2\\sqrt{5} \\) cm into the formula:\n\n\\[ d = (2\\sqrt{5})\\sqrt{2} = 2\\sqrt{10} = 5\\sqrt{2} \\]\n\nThus, the length of \\( AC \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{5\\sqrt{2}} \\]"
