"The general form of a sine function is \\( y = A \\sin(Bx + C) \\), where \\( A \\) is the amplitude, \\( B \\) affects the period, and \\( C \\) affects the phase shift.\n\nFor the given function \\( y = 2 \\sin \\left( x + \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) \\):\n- The amplitude \\( A \\) is 2.\n- The coefficient \\( B \\) is 1.\n- The coefficient \\( C \\) is \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\).\n\nThe phase shift of a sine function \\( y = A \\sin(Bx + C) \\) is given by \\( -\\frac{C}{B} \\).\n\nTherefore, the phase shift is:\n\\[\n-\\frac{C}{B} = -\\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}{1} = -\\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSince the phase shift is negative, it indicates a phase shift to the left.\n\nThus, the phase shift is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\). I hope it is correct."
"We start by simplifying the given expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sec^4 \\alpha}{\\tan^2 \\beta} + \\frac{\\sec^4 \\beta}{\\tan^2 \\alpha}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\sec^2 \\theta = \\frac{1}{\\tan^2 \\theta}\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\tan^2 \\alpha}}{\\tan^2 \\beta} + \\frac{\\frac{1}{\\tan^2 \\beta}}{\\tan^2 \\alpha}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying each term, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\tan^2 \\alpha \\tan^2 \\beta} + \\frac{1}{\\tan^2 \\beta \\tan^2 \\alpha}\n\\]\n\nNotice that \\(\\frac{1}{\\tan^2 \\alpha \\tan^2 \\beta} = \\frac{1}{\\tan^2 \\beta \\tan^2 \\alpha}\\) because the terms are the same, so this simplifies to:\n\\[\n1 + 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of the expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]"
"First, we need to find the values of \\(\\sin 10^\\circ\\) and \\(\\sin 70^\\circ\\).\n\nUsing trigonometric values:\n\\[\n\\sin 10^\\circ \\approx 0.1736\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin 70^\\circ \\approx 0.9397\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these values into the expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2 \\sin 10^\\circ} - 2 \\sin 70^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2 \\cdot 0.1736} - 2 \\cdot 0.9397\n\\]\n\nCalculate each term:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{0.3472} \\approx 2.828\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 0.9397 \\approx 1.8794\n\\]\n\nNow, subtract the two results:\n\\[\n2.828 - 1.8794 = 0.9486\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.9486}\n\\]"
"We have that\n\\[\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix},\\]so $\\mathbf{A}^3 = \\mathbf{I},$ where $\\mathbf{I}$ is the identity matrix. Therefore,\n\\[\\mathbf{A}^{27} = (\\mathbf{A}^3)^9 \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{I},\\]\n\\[\\mathbf{A}^{31} = (\\mathbf{A}^3)^{10} \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{I},\\]\n\\[\\mathbf{A}^{40} = (\\mathbf{A}^3)^{13} \\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{I}.\\]Thus,\n\\[\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40} = \\mathbf{I} + \\mathbf{I} + \\mathbf{I} = \\boxed{3\\mathbf{I}}.\\]\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $3\\mathbf{I}$, which is 3 times the identity matrix. I hope it is correct."
"To find the matrix that corresponds to reflecting over the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can use the formula for the reflection matrix over a line perpendicular to a vector \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\end{pmatrix}\\):\n\n\\[\nR = \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} 2b & -a \\\\ -a & -2b \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(a = -1\\) and \\(b = 3\\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nR = \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} 2(3) & -(-1) \\\\ -(-1) & -2(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\n\\]\nTherefore, the reflection matrix is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]"
