"Since $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are orthogonal, we have $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0.$\n\nGiven that\n$$\\operatorname{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 13 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix},$$\nwe know that\n$$\\frac{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 13 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}}{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}} \\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are orthogonal, we have $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0.$\n\nTherefore,\n$$\\operatorname{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 13 \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 13 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$."
"The normal vector of the plane $P$ is the same as the first column of the projection matrix. Therefore, the first column of the given matrix is\n\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{13}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{14} \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n\nTo express the normal vector in the form \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\end{pmatrix}\\) where \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are integers, \\(a > 0\\), and \\(\\gcd(|a|,|b|,|c|) = 1\\), we multiply each component by 14 to clear the denominators:\n\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n\nNext, we find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of the absolute values of the components:\n\n\\[\\gcd(13, 2, 3) = 1.\\]\n\nThus, the vector is already in the required form. Therefore, the normal vector of the plane $P$ is\n\n\\[\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}}.\\]"
